INSERT INTO course_modules (id, course_id, section_id, title, type, position, content_body, estimated_minutes, knowledge_check)
VALUES ('249e45fb-7fd3-51d3-a20b-9fd0827fd6df', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'd3f8c476-a30c-5ea7-b497-dc818ddabea5', 'Mentorship Story', 'notes', 23, $md$## Why interviewers ask this

This checks whether you can grow other people, and not only yourself: a signal companies weight heavily for senior and staff-track roles, and increasingly for anyone who'll eventually onboard a junior teammate. It also reveals whether you can adapt your explanation style to someone else's level, not just your own.

## The framework: STAR, weighted on their growth, not your effort

- **Situation**: who you mentored and what gap they had.
- **Task**: what you specifically took responsibility for teaching or unblocking.
- **Action**: your actual method: pairing, code review comments, a specific technique for letting them find the answer rather than just giving it to them.
- **Result**: evidence of their growth, specifically something they can now do independently that they couldn't before, more than just "they said thanks."

The common weak version of this story is "I helped a junior engineer with their code" with no specifics about what changed in them. The strong version names a before/after in their capability.

## Worked example

> **Situation**: "A new grad on our team was technically capable but afraid to open PRs for anything beyond trivial changes. She'd sit on finished work for days second-guessing it. **Task**: I wasn't her manager, but I noticed the pattern in standup and decided to pair with her directly. **Action**: I started reviewing her draft PRs before she opened them publicly, but instead of fixing things myself, I'd leave comments like 'what happens if this list is empty?' and let her find the fix. After a few weeks I stopped offering to pre-review and told her I trusted her to open PRs directly. She pushed back, so I pointed to the last five PRs, where my comments had been minor at most. **Result**: Her PR turnaround time from 'code complete' to 'opened' dropped from days to hours within a month, and six months later she was doing the same pre-review-by-questions technique for the next new hire on the team, which is when I knew it had actually stuck rather than just made her dependent on me."

The story ends with her teaching someone else. That's the real proof of growth, not gratitude.

## Your template

```
Situation: [who you mentored, and the specific gap]
Task: [what you took responsibility for, even informally]
Action: [your method: questions over answers, pairing, staged independence]
Result: [concrete change in their capability] + [evidence it stuck without you]
```

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Name a specific before/after in their skill | Say "I helped them a lot" with no specifics |
| Show a method that builds independence, not dependence | Describe just doing the work for them |
| End with evidence the growth stuck after you stepped back | End at "they were grateful" |
$md$, 15, $json$[]$json$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id, title=EXCLUDED.title, type=EXCLUDED.type, content_body=EXCLUDED.content_body, position=EXCLUDED.position, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, knowledge_check=EXCLUDED.knowledge_check, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO course_modules (id, course_id, section_id, title, type, position, content_body, estimated_minutes, knowledge_check)
VALUES ('050bf69f-d43e-5dd6-83b7-52f8a8695b2b', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'd3f8c476-a30c-5ea7-b497-dc818ddabea5', 'Difficult Coworker Story', 'notes', 24, $md$## Why interviewers ask this

This is different from the Day 4 conflict story, which was about disagreeing on a technical decision. This one is about personality and working-style friction: someone who was dismissive, took credit, or missed commitments repeatedly. Interviewers want to know you can stay professional and keep shipping when you can't just out-argue the problem with data.

## The framework: STAR, weighted on professionalism under friction

- **Situation**: the specific behavior pattern, described factually, not as a character assassination.
- **Task**: how their behavior was actually affecting your work or the team's.
- **Action**: what you did: usually a direct, private conversation first, escalation only if that didn't work, and continuing to collaborate professionally regardless of your personal feelings.
- **Result**: the outcome, even if only partial. Not every difficult-coworker story ends in a full turnaround, and pretending it does can read as dishonest.

The trap here is nearly identical to Day 4: don't make the other person a villain, and don't spend the story venting. Interviewers are evaluating your behavior, not judging theirs.

## Worked example

> **Situation**: "I worked with an engineer on another team who'd regularly commit to API contract changes in meetings, then not follow through, which broke my team's integration work without warning at least three times over two months. **Task**: I needed the integration to actually stay stable, and escalating immediately felt like it would burn a relationship I'd need long-term. **Action**: I asked him directly, one-on-one, whether the commitments in meetings were realistic or if something upstream was blocking him. It turned out his team was chronically understaffed and he was over-committing under pressure from his own manager. I suggested we move from verbal commitments to a shared doc with actual dates, so at minimum I'd get advance warning instead of a broken integration. **Result**: The surprise breakages stopped. He still occasionally missed dates, but now I had lead time to adjust instead of finding out from a failing test. I didn't fix his team's staffing problem, but I fixed the part of it that was actually my problem to fix."

The honest "I didn't fix everything, but I fixed my piece of it" ending is more credible than a fake full resolution.

## Your template

```
Situation: [the specific friction, described factually]
Task: [how it was actually affecting your work]
Action: [private conversation first] → [what you learned/proposed] → [escalation only if needed]
Result: [the outcome, even if partial], no invented full resolution
```

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Describe behavior factually, not as a personality attack | Turn the story into venting about the person |
| Show you tried a direct conversation before escalating | Jump straight to going over their head |
| Admit if the resolution was only partial | Fabricate a tidy happy ending |
$md$, 15, $json$[]$json$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id, title=EXCLUDED.title, type=EXCLUDED.type, content_body=EXCLUDED.content_body, position=EXCLUDED.position, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, knowledge_check=EXCLUDED.knowledge_check, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO course_modules (id, course_id, section_id, title, type, position, content_body, estimated_minutes, knowledge_check)
VALUES ('90f99476-a44f-5c29-b23b-5ba207da71b6', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'd3f8c476-a30c-5ea7-b497-dc818ddabea5', 'Career Goals', 'notes', 25, $md$## Why interviewers ask this

"Where do you see yourself in 5 years?" isn't asking you to predict the future. It's checking two things: will you stay long enough to be worth hiring, and does your trajectory actually make sense with this role. An answer with nothing to do with the job you're interviewing for signals you're not actually aligned with it.

## The framework: near-term concrete, long-term directional

**1-year goal**: specific and achievable, a skill deepened, a scope of ownership, a type of project. This should sound like something the role you're interviewing for would directly produce.

**5-year goal**: directional, not a job-title ladder. "I want to be a staff engineer" is weaker than "I want to be the person a team turns to for system design decisions in [domain]," because the second is about capability, not a title, and holds up regardless of how the company levels people.

**The connective thread**: explicitly tie both back to why this role is a reasonable step toward them, not a random data point in your career.

Avoid two failure modes: goals so vague they could apply to anyone ("I want to keep growing and learning"), and goals that don't need this specific job at all (management-track goals in an IC interview, founder goals in an interview at a large company).

## Worked example

> "In the next year, I want to go deeper on distributed systems specifically. Right now I've worked adjacent to sharding and replication but haven't owned that kind of design decision end-to-end, which is exactly the kind of work this backend platform team does. In five years, I want to be the engineer people bring hard data-consistency problems to before they've fully scoped them. Not necessarily a management track; I want to stay hands-on, but with enough depth that I'm shaping architecture decisions, not just implementing them. This role is a direct step toward that because the platform team owns exactly the kind of infrastructure decisions I want more reps on."

Notice the 5-year goal is a capability, not a title, and both years connect explicitly back to this specific role.

## Your template

```
1-year: I want to [specific skill/scope], which is exactly what [this role] would give me through [specific project/team responsibility].

5-year: I want to be [capability-based description, not just a title], [what that would look like day to day].

Connection: This role is a step toward that because [specific, honest link].
```

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Make the 1-year goal concrete and role-relevant | Say "I want to keep growing and learning" |
| Frame the 5-year goal around capability, not title | Recite a generic career ladder |
| Tie both goals explicitly to this role | Describe a trajectory this job wouldn't advance |
$md$, 15, $json$[]$json$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id, title=EXCLUDED.title, type=EXCLUDED.type, content_body=EXCLUDED.content_body, position=EXCLUDED.position, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, knowledge_check=EXCLUDED.knowledge_check, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO course_modules (id, course_id, section_id, title, type, position, content_body, estimated_minutes, knowledge_check)
VALUES ('a62acd41-d705-561d-be9a-23635eceba47', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'd3f8c476-a30c-5ea7-b497-dc818ddabea5', 'Asking for Help Story', 'notes', 26, $md$## Why interviewers ask this

This question filters for two failure modes at once: engineers who never ask for help and burn days stuck on something a five-minute conversation would fix, and engineers who ask for help on everything and never develop independent judgment. Interviewers want evidence you know where that line is.

## The framework: STAR, weighted on the decision point

- **Situation**: what you were stuck on.
- **Task**: what you'd already tried on your own before asking, which is the part that proves you didn't reach for help reflexively.
- **Action**: specifically how you asked: who you went to, and, importantly, how you framed the question so it was easy for them to help quickly rather than dumping the whole problem on them.
- **Result**: what you learned, and how it changed your instinct for when to ask going forward.

## Worked example

> **Situation**: "I was debugging a memory leak in a long-running worker process and had spent about four hours narrowing it to somewhere in our caching layer, with no further progress. **Task**: I had a choice between grinding for another few hours or asking someone who'd built the original caching layer. **Action**: I went to the engineer who wrote it, but instead of saying 'the cache is leaking, help,' I came with what I'd already ruled out: it wasn't the eviction policy, wasn't the TTL logic, memory grew steadily even with zero new keys. Then I asked a specific question: 'is there a reference being held somewhere outside the cache map itself?' That took him about two minutes to answer: a metrics callback was capturing a closure over cache entries that outlived their TTL. **Result**: I fixed it within the hour instead of losing another afternoon, and it changed how I approach getting stuck. Now I set a rough time box (about 2-3 hours for a non-urgent bug), and when I do ask, I always bring what I've ruled out so the other person's time goes toward the actual gap in my knowledge, not re-treading my steps."

The specificity of what was ruled out is what separates "I asked for help" from "I asked for help well."

## Your template

```
Situation: [what you were stuck on]
Task: [what you'd already tried before deciding to ask]
Action: [who you asked] + [how you framed the question: what you'd ruled out, what you specifically needed]
Result: [what you learned] + [how it changed your instinct for when to ask]
```

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Show what you tried before asking | Make it sound like you asked immediately |
| Frame the ask so it's fast for the other person to answer | Dump the whole problem with no context |
| Note a concrete rule for when you ask now | End without any lasting takeaway |
$md$, 15, $json$[]$json$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET section_id=EXCLUDED.section_id, title=EXCLUDED.title, type=EXCLUDED.type, content_body=EXCLUDED.content_body, position=EXCLUDED.position, estimated_minutes=EXCLUDED.estimated_minutes, knowledge_check=EXCLUDED.knowledge_check, updated_at=now();

INSERT INTO course_modules (id, course_id, section_id, title, type, position, content_body, estimated_minutes, knowledge_check)
VALUES ('4f02fb82-de10-572f-9d22-c231140c277b', '57f5e0f7-67b7-55ab-a3e7-469947105cd5', 'd3f8c476-a30c-5ea7-b497-dc818ddabea5', 'Feedback Story', 'notes', 27, $md$## Why interviewers ask this

How you react to being told you're wrong, in the moment and afterward, predicts how coachable you'll be on the job. Interviewers aren't looking for a story where the feedback was gentle and you nodded along. They're looking for feedback that actually stung, and proof you changed something real afterward instead of just saying "noted."

## The framework: STAR, weighted on the actual behavior change

- **Situation**: the feedback itself, stated plainly, including that it was hard to hear.
- **Task**: the context that made it land the way it did.
- **Action**: your immediate reaction (ideally: you didn't get defensive) and the concrete thing you changed afterward.
- **Result**: evidence the change stuck, ideally confirmed by someone else noticing, not just your own self-report.

The failure mode here is picking feedback that's actually a humblebrag ("my manager said I take on too much"). Pick something that was genuinely hard to hear and that you genuinely changed in response to.

## Worked example

> **Situation**: "My manager told me in a 1:1 that my PR comments came across as blunt to the point of discouraging. She'd gotten feedback from two people on the team that they dreaded getting reviews from me, even though my technical calls were usually right. **Task**: This was hard to hear because I thought I was just being direct and efficient, not unkind. **Action**: I didn't argue the point in the moment. I asked her for a specific example instead of defending myself generally, and she pointed to a comment where I'd written 'this is wrong, use X instead' with no explanation. I started rewriting review comments to include the why, not just the what, and to phrase things as questions where I wasn't 100% certain: 'have you considered X, since Y?' instead of flat corrections. **Result**: Three months later, in my next round of peer feedback, the same two people specifically mentioned my reviews had gotten easier to receive, without me prompting them or bringing it up. That's when I knew it had actually changed something, not just softened my own self-image of it."

The peer feedback three months later, unprompted, is what proves the change was real and not performative.

## Your template

```
Situation: [the feedback itself, stated plainly], [why it was hard to hear]
Task: [context that made it land this way]
Action: [your immediate reaction: no defensiveness] + [the concrete thing you changed]
Result: [evidence the change stuck], ideally confirmed by someone else, unprompted
```

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Pick feedback that genuinely stung | Pick a disguised brag as your "hard feedback" |
| Show you didn't get defensive in the moment | Argue the point in your retelling of the story |
| Give evidence the change actually stuck | Say "I took it to heart" with nothing concrete after |
$md$, 15, $json$[]$json$::jsonb)
